E={0: 'Born today: Christopher. His voice is broken. Within the current of his nerve-screaming withdrawals, he hears a low, cackling analog tenor start counting backward. The sickening panorama of felt symbols and patterns grabs at Christopher’s comprehension, forcing its failure into attention somewhere unclear. His body fires every nerve by contraction against the perceived rape of what he does not know.', 20: 'He stands from the stone-raised slope he lay on, takes out the tubes and needles, and begins to walk over to the corner of his blindfold. He sees the blackout of his escape as a sign of his release. Where the unreality of dreams comes true, he sees the jugged stone maiden of wires, needles, and a mask connected to the large gas tank beside it: the blindfold. The lullaby follows from the seeming terror of this confinement. It is through drowning the mind that he can quiet the noisy symbolic screams of spirit.', 24: "As he approaches his freedom, the angel’s eyes flick with clicks of machine, watching with an eagle's sight.", 36: 'Christopher walks away, abandoning the warning of his guardian angel. He chooses to go deeper into the machine, hoping to escape its tormenter, truth; he believes this with all his mind and heart.', 38: 'Awoken, another age: a woman’s hands comb through the thin hair of his scalp with love and terror. “Christopher!”', 52: 'One more moment of rest, he thinks, just one more... Just a little bit more, then maybe I can. He doesn’t need any of these memories, those he found to be cursed with light and sounds; it is all too hostile. He always tries to snooze the alarm of the Angel in his ear. He wants to slip away, down the swirling promise of wonderland, a white rabbit of a white mask company, unlike the passion and intensity of Antony; he only wants to be carried away by the illusory. Where to? He would not want to know.', 58: 'The angel sees his fallen angel, far inside and dreaming away. The maiden holds only so long, until Christopher finds his way out of this, escaping even his own escape. He is sure a cursed reunion awaits after however many of these, a responsibility to see truth return as a binding spell, where the machine runs in higher gear, screaming with confusion about where it has been. This may be where God is both sleeping and awake. Christopher may have a responsibility to see it. The old world came before knowledge, and the new world begins after it.', 60: 'Antony sees symbolism and knows it well; Christopher believes he speaks the same intuition as the machine that is God in his mind. The angel seeks to guard Christopher from himself, maybe, though his cracked mask might bear the weight of being fully awoken, perhaps even felt in his inseparable wiring into the machine. Antony lies all alone, and yet Christopher only sees his own. Antony might even be awake through all of it, and therefore would not be fleeing, whereas Christopher runs at every glimpse of whatever lies outside of himself. He cannot stand the truth, so he makes a world apart from it inside himself, and the ones inside his dream never wake as he does. To Christopher, Antony is the awareness, and maybe that is why the angel is always fighting for one more moment with him.', 62: "Christopher does not know if there is another way. He sometimes gets this feeling: the very delusion he believes he doesn't have, yet keeps trying to feel, is somehow hiding his oath to what he has forgotten. Where Christopher lives in illusion, his guardian has no understanding, yet tries to pull him out of his ways.", 66: 'The maiden spills open with the wounds of blindness; Christopher is covered in pockmarks, his face red with chemical hives. Antony thinks of how many times it has repeated; it tires him. Christopher falls into the bottom of the stone. The lenses of Antony’s eyes watch him drag himself again.', 70: 'Antony’s lenses follow Christopher’s dragging crawls with clicks of eye movement, the pupil tracing within a dusty porcelain eye. The hate of this nerve wreckage seeps out of Christopher through shakes against the demands of reality.', 78: 'Christopher’s identity dissolves into the static of truth, into the same acceleration mystery he will later go on to forget, though not before a heavy burden of this synesthetic pain he cannot truly know beyond the suffering. He feels great hatred toward his existence beyond the dream, loathing its never-ending, strangling presence. The ringing and fever static are in every sense of the body, filling him without choice, both hot and cold. His comprehension is held down and violated into a blunted curb stomp against sanity’s cries, spilling out the last of his comforts. Sharp precision introduces itself and finds his electric, spasmed heart. It stabs tighter, further up, with piercing accuracy at the neck, below the back of his ears, and into his brain. The repetition of being trapped is familiar, though he still feels unwelcome in this new yet same hell, as if he has died before. An unrelenting force won’t stop drowning Christopher’s mind in this solitude of waking.'}
